{"tool_name":"snaphic","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"snaphic","home":"https://github.com/HuMingLab/SnapHiC","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"A computational pipeline to identify chromatin loops from single-cell Hi-C data.","addition_date":"2021-12-13T23:16:27.439220Z","last_update_date":"2021-12-13T23:16:27.441855Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:snaphic","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"biren@health.ucsd.edu","name":"Bing Ren","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5435-1127","typeEntity":"Person"},{"email":"hum@ccf.org","name":"Ming Hu","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0987-2916","typeEntity":"Person"},{"name":"Armen Abnousi","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1822-0928"},{"name":"Quan Sun","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8324-2803"},{"name":"Yanxiao Zhang","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9618-637X"},{"name":"Yuchen Yang","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5977-1617"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Filtering","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3695"},{"term":"Imputation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3557"},{"term":"Standardisation and normalisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3435"}]}],"name":"SnapHiC","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Jennifer","publication":[{"doi":"10.1038/S41592-021-01231-2","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2021, The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature America, Inc.Single-cell Hi-C (scHi-C) analysis has been increasingly used to map chromatin architecture in diverse tissue contexts, but computational tools to define chromatin loops at high resolution from scHi-C data are still lacking. Here, we describe Single-Nucleus Analysis Pipeline for Hi-C (SnapHiC), a method that can identify chromatin loops at high resolution and accuracy from scHi-C data. Using scHi-C data from 742 mouse embryonic stem cells, we benchmark SnapHiC against a number of computational tools developed for mapping chromatin loops and interactions from bulk Hi-C. We further demonstrate its use by analyzing single-nucleus methyl-3C-seq data from 2,869 human prefrontal cortical cells, which uncovers cell type-specific chromatin loops and predicts putative target genes for noncoding sequence variants associated with neuropsychiatric disorders. Our results indicate that SnapHiC could facilitate the analysis of cell type-specific chromatin architecture and gene regulatory programs in complex tissues.","authors":[{"name":"Abnousi A."},{"name":"Chen Z."},{"name":"Fang R."},{"name":"Hu M."},{"name":"Lagler T.M."},{"name":"Lee L."},{"name":"Li G."},{"name":"Li Y."},{"name":"Ren B."},{"name":"Sun Q."},{"name":"Wen J."},{"name":"Yang Y."},{"name":"Yu M."},{"name":"Zhang Y."}],"date":"2021-09-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nature Methods","title":"SnapHiC: a computational pipeline to identify chromatin loops from single-cell Hi-C data"},"pmcid":"PMC8440170","pmid":"34446921"}],"topic":[{"term":"ChIP-on-chip","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3179"},{"term":"ChIP-seq","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3169"},{"term":"Epigenomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3173"},{"term":"Mapping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0102"},{"term":"Workflows","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0769"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"SnapHiC","home":"https://bio.tools/snaphic","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"A computational pipeline to identify chromatin loops from single-cell Hi-C data.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:snaphic/cmd/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":null,"registration":null},"packages":null,"source_packages":false,"sourcecode":{"free":null,"interpreted":true,"license":null,